%S A398324 4,8,16,20,24,28,34,36,40,44,48,56,60,64,66,68,72,76,80,88,90,94,102,
%T A398324 104,110,114,116,124,128,132,134,136,140,144,148,152,154,160,164,166,
%U A398324 168,174,176,180,184,188,192,194,200,202,204,208,212,220,224,228,230,232,236,240,244,252,254,258
%N A398324 Positions k such that the prefix of length k of the Kolakoski sequence contains an even number of 1's and an even number of 2's.
%C A398324 Let N_1(m) and N_2(m) denote, respectively, the number of 1's and 2's among A000002(1), ..., A000002(m). This is the sequence of positive integers m such that both N_1(m) and N_2(m) are even.
%C A398324 Since N_1(m) + N_2(m) = m, equivalently the sequence terms are the even values of m for which N_1(m) is even.
%C A398324 All terms are even.
%C A398324 This sequence is infinite. Indeed, suppose that from some point on no even position m satisfies the defining condition. Then, for every sufficiently large even m, the number of 1's in K(1),...,K(m) is odd. Hence the number of 1's in each pair K(m+1),K(m+2) is even, so K(m+1)=K(m+2). Thus, from some point on, K consists of equal pairs. Since every run of the Kolakoski sequence has length 1 or 2, consecutive equal pairs cannot have the same value; consequently, from some point on all runs have length 2. The run-length sequence of K would therefore be eventually constant 2. But K is equal to its own run-length sequence, so K itself would be eventually constant 2, a contradiction. Hence there are infinitely many prefixes containing an even number of 1's and an even number of 2's.
%e A398324 The prefix of length 4 of A000002 is 1221 and contains two 1's and two 2's, so a(1) = 4.
%e A398324 The prefix of length 6 of A000002 is 122112 and contains three 1's and three 2's, so 6 is not a term of a(n).
%e A398324 The prefix of length 8 of A000002 is 12211212 and contains four 1's and four 2's, so a(2) = 8.
%t A398324 KolakoskiEvenPrefixLengths[N_] := Module[{s = {1, 2, 2}, i = 3, x = 1, ones = 1, r = {}},While[Length[r] < N,Do[AppendTo[s, x];If[x == 1, ones++];If[EvenQ[Length[s]] && EvenQ[ones], AppendTo[r, Length[s]]],{s[[i]]}];x = 3 - x;i++];Take[r, N]]
%Y A398324 Cf. A000002 (Kolakoski sequence).
%Y A398324 Cf. A022292, the positions n for which the prefix of length n of A000002 contains equally many 1's and 2's. The terms of A022292 for which n is divisible by 4 form a subsequence of the present sequence.
%Y A398324 Cf. A074273 (positions in A000002 where there are an even number of 1's and the current term is 1).
%Y A398324 Cf. A088568 (twice the excess of 1's over 2's in prefixes of the Kolakoski sequence).
%K A398324 nonn,easy,new
